(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-host-defenses-nonspecific-quiz"]={"title":"Host Defenses: Nonspecific Mechanisms — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What percentage of white cells are NEUTROPHILS, and what is their role?","choices":["1 to 3 per cent; they are phagocytes","20 to 35 per cent; they are phagocytes","0.5 per cent; they release chemical mediators","55 to 90 per cent; they are phagocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — lobed nuclei with lavender granules. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is a DAMP, as distinct from a PAMP?","choices":["A receptor on the macrophage surface","A cytokine released by damaged cells","A damage-associated molecular pattern","A pattern found only on bacteria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: PAMPs mark non-self, DAMPs mark altered self. It signals cells that are damaged, stressed or virus-invaded. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which cytokine besides tumour necrosis factor alpha is named as increasing temperature?","choices":["Interferon beta","Interleukin 12","Interleukin 6","CXCL8"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How does interleukin 1 beta escape the macrophage, and what happens to the cell?","choices":["Through pores; the cell dies by pyroptosis","It is exported by vesicle and the macrophage survives","It diffuses freely across the membrane","It is released only after phagocytosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — release costs the cell its life. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How does fever act against BACTERIA specifically?","choices":["It lyses their cell walls","It blocks their adhesion to mucosa","It starves them of iron","It denatures their ribosomes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — iron starvation is the antibacterial mechanism. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which cell works as infection begins, and which waits to be called?","choices":["The dendritic cell works first and calls both","The macrophage works first and raises the alarm","The neutrophil works first and calls the macrophage","Both arrive simultaneously"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil waits for it to enter tissue. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which molecule is by far the most important in the complement cascade?","choices":["C1","C5","C9","C3"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the pathways differ mainly in how they get to it. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Why does antibody defeat the sialic acid evasion strategy?","choices":["Antibody prevents C5 cleavage","Antibody masks the sialic acid first","Antibody cleaves sialic acid enzymatically","Antibody inactivates factor H directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — so these bacteria resist complement ONLY when no specific antibody is present. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What happens to albumin in the acute-phase response?","choices":["It is converted into C-reactive protein","It DECREASES, while defensive plasma proteins increase","It increases along with the defensive proteins","It is unchanged"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the liver shifts its output. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Why is C-reactive protein used in diagnosis?","choices":["It falls predictably in infection","It identifies the organism responsible","It rises over a hundredfold","It is specific to bacterial infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a large, measurable swing. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What do PROTEASE INHIBITORS do, and how much of serum protein are they?","choices":["Form membrane pores — a trace of serum protein","Block microbial proteases that degrade human tissue","Digest bacterial cell walls — about half of serum proteins","Opsonise pathogens — about one third of serum proteins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; alpha-2 macroglobulin is the example given. About one tenth of all serum proteins. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What are the two roles of C5a?","choices":["It opsonises pathogens and forms pores","It inhibits complement and clears debris","It initiates the membrane attack complex","It recruits neutrophils"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — chemotaxis plus inflammation. It is the most potent anaphylatoxin. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Where can pattern recognition receptors be located?","choices":["Only on the plasma membrane","Only in the nucleus","Only on circulating antibody","On the plasma membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — location determines what they can sample. In the cytosol, or in endosomes. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What does the lacrimal apparatus contribute beyond washing the eye?","choices":["Interferon","Defensins from Paneth cells","Lysozyme, a chemical defence","Complement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — so tears are a physical AND a chemical barrier. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What do natural killer cells kill?","choices":["Only extracellular parasites","Cells infected by viruses","Free bacteria in plasma","Only cancer cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — cytotoxic against infected cells. Bacteria or protozoan parasites. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Where do encounters between host defenses and invaders take place?","choices":["Inside epithelial cells only","The bone marrow and thymus only","The reticuloendothelial system","The bloodstream only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the four compartments named. Extracellular fluid, bloodstream and lymphatic system. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"How do natural killer cells and macrophages cooperate?","choices":["Natural killer cells recruit macrophages, which return interleukin 12","They compete for the same targets","They do not interact","Macrophages recruit and activate natural killer cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a reciprocal loop at the infection site. Those return interferon gamma, enhancing macrophage phagocytosis. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"Which organisms colonize the epidermis as normal flora?","choices":["Coagulase-negative staphylococci and coryneform bacteria","Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes","Escherichia coli and Enterococcus faecalis","Candida albicans and Aspergillus niger"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — nonpathogenic residents. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How do extracellular and intracellular pathogens differ in what defense they need?","choices":["Defence varies accordingly","Both are handled identically","Only extracellular pathogens can be defended against","Intracellular pathogens are always cleared by antibody"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the same organism may need both kinds of defence over its life cycle. Some organisms are intracellular at one stage and extracellular at another. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does interferon actually do to a virus?","choices":["Opsonises virus for phagocytosis","Nothing directly","Lyses the viral envelope","Digests viral nucleic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Interferon does not kill viruses; it stops spread to surrounding tissue. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How do factor H and factor I work together?","choices":["Factor H makes C3b susceptible to cleavage by factor I","Factor H cleaves C3b and factor I binds the product","Both increase complement activation","Both form part of the membrane attack complex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a two-step brake. Creating iC3b which cannot form a convertase. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"By how much do C-reactive protein and serum amyloid A rise?","choices":["Over a hundredfold","About twofold","About tenfold","They fall rather than rise"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — which is why C-reactive protein is used diagnostically. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which proteins prevent soluble C5b, C6 and C7 associating with human membranes?","choices":["S protein, clusterin and factor J","CD59 and homologous restriction factor","Decay accelerating factor and membrane cofactor protein","Factor H and factor I"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which of these is a use of the differential white count?","choices":["Detecting antibody titre","Diagnosing infection types","Measuring clotting time","Assessing renal function"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — six uses. Inflammation, allergies, immune disorders, leukaemia and myelodysplastic syndrome. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What distinguishes the CD56 dim from the CD56 bright natural killer cell?","choices":["CD56 dim secretes cytokines; CD56 bright kills","Both only kill","Both only secrete cytokines","CD56 dim kills virally infected cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — two effector functions in subpopulations adapted to their tissue. CD56 bright secretes cytokines to maintain inflammation. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"How does interferon protect uninfected cells?","choices":["It presents viral antigen to T cells","It induces antiviral proteins in them","It coats them in sialic acid","It forms pores in their membranes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it prepares the neighbours. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What does interferon GAMMA do?","choices":["Inhibits cancer, stimulates B cells, activates macrophages","Activates natural killer cells","Assists B and T cell maturation","Blocks the complement cascade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and it increases macrophage effectiveness. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What makes up the FIRST line of defense?","choices":["Barriers at the portal of entry","Protective cells and antimicrobial substances","Antibodies and defensive cell lines","Complement and interferon alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and all of it is nonspecific — it blocks invasion before entry. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which fragment initiates the membrane attack complex?","choices":["C3a","C3b","C5b","C5a"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Besides phagocytosis, what three roles do monocytes and macrophages have?","choices":["Antibody production, memory, and complement fixation","Clotting, chemotaxis, and opsonisation","Histamine release, parasite killing, and mucus production","Cellular housekeeping, antigen presentation to lymphocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cytokine secretion. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 67"}]};
